The iterator keeps a raw `*mut T` at the start of the not-yet-yielded window plus a remaining count. The original `&mut [T]` is consumed into it, so the only live borrows into the buffer are the references the iterator hands out. Each `next()` reborrows that exclusive access as two `&mut T` into the two adjacent one-element regions `[ptr, ptr+1)` and `[ptr+1, ptr+2)` — disjoint, so no aliasing (the same guarantee `split_at_mut`/`chunks_mut` give, which we're not allowed to call) — then advances the window by 2. Since the window only moves forward and every element belongs to at most one `[ptr, ptr+2)` range, nothing is ever handed out twice, and the odd trailing element is simply never yielded.

```rust
use std::marker::PhantomData;

/// Iterator over the non-overlapping consecutive pairs of a mutable slice.
///
/// For a slice of length `n`, yields exactly `n / 2` disjoint pairs and drops
/// the odd trailing element, if any. Every element is handed out at most once.
pub struct PairsMut<'a, T> {
    /// First element of the not-yet-yielded window.
    ptr: *mut T,
    /// Number of not-yet-yielded elements.
    len: usize,
    _marker: PhantomData<&'a mut T>,
}

/// Iterate over `s` as non-overlapping consecutive `(&mut T, &mut T)` pairs.
pub fn pairs_mut<'a, T>(s: &'a mut [T]) -> PairsMut<'a, T> {
    PairsMut {
        ptr: s.as_mut_ptr(),
        len: s.len(),
        _marker: PhantomData,
    }
}

impl<'a, T> Iterator for PairsMut<'a, T> {
    type Item = (&'a mut T, &'a mut T);

    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item> {
        // A pair needs two remaining elements; otherwise we're finished.
        if self.len < 2 {
            return None;
        }

        // SAFETY:
        // - `self.ptr` lies inside the original slice, which is alive for `'a`
        //   (the caller's `&'a mut [T]` was moved into us) and is `T`-aligned
        //   because it came from `as_mut_ptr`.
        // - `self.len >= 2` guarantees both `self.ptr` and `self.ptr.add(1)`
        //   are in bounds.
        // - The one-element regions `[ptr, ptr+1)` and `[ptr+1, ptr+2)` are
        //   disjoint, so the two `&mut T` references cannot alias. This is the
        //   same guarantee `split_at_mut`/`chunks_mut` give internally, which
        //   we are forbidden from calling.
        let first = unsafe { &mut *self.ptr };
        let second = unsafe { &mut *self.ptr.add(1) };

        // Advance the window past the pair we just handed out. The remaining
        // window `[ptr+2, ..)` is disjoint from `first` and `second`, so the
        // references we returned never alias anything yielded later.
        self.ptr = self.ptr.add(2);
        self.len -= 2;

        Some((first, second))
    }

    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, usize) {
        // Exactly `len / 2` pairs remain; the odd tail is dropped.
        let n = self.len / 2;
        (n, n)
    }
}

impl<T> ExactSizeIterator for PairsMut<'_, T> {}
impl<T> FusedIterator for PairsMut<'_, T> {}
